{"nodes": [{"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_pricing___monetization_psychology", "label": "Pricing & Monetization Psychology", "file_type": "document", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "document", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_use_a_disfluent_font_for_genuine_deals___and_name_tiers_for_the_mix_you_want", "label": "Use a disfluent font for genuine deals \u2014 and name tiers for the mix you want", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Use a disfluent font for genuine deals \u2014 and name tiers for the mix you want", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A uniformly polished pricing page can be easy to scan yet weak at selling. For a genuinely favorable promotion, a disfluent (ugly) font on the price can make people process the number more deeply. Tier labels can change purchase incidence and which tier buyers choose \u2014 and those effects can run in opposite directions."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_price_from_cost_margin_floor_and_value_ceiling___never_tarot_or_competitor_minus_20_", "label": "Price from cost+margin floor and value ceiling \u2014 never tarot or competitor-minus-20%", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Price from cost+margin floor and value ceiling \u2014 never tarot or competitor-minus-20%", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Two calculations give your floor and ceiling. Floor = cost to serve + target margin (infra, support, tools, tokens; if R$10/account and 80% margin, floor is R$50). But two traps: price too low signals low quality before use, and CAC math can eat the margin (CAC R$100 + margin R$40/mo = 2.5-month payback; churn at month 3 = you worked 3 months for R$20). Ceiling = reference value \u2014 what the client pays today to solve it their way (Excel + 1.5h/day of admin; or the loss they absorb by not solving)."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_the_freemium_trap__in_numbers__higher_conversion__far_lower_cash", "label": "The freemium trap, in numbers: higher conversion, far lower cash", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: The freemium trap, in numbers: higher conversion, far lower cash", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A free plan lifts signup conversion but can crush the economics \u2014 the higher top-of-funnel number hides a worse business."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_whether_freemium_works_is_decided_by_the_cost_to_serve_a_free_user", "label": "Whether freemium works is decided by the cost to serve a free user", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Whether freemium works is decided by the cost to serve a free user", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Freemium isn't good or bad in the abstract \u2014 the *cost of free* decides, and it hinges on (1) how much it costs to serve non-payers and (2) how long/expensive activation is."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_price_is_the_cheapest_money___stop_anchoring_it_to_the_cheapest_competitor", "label": "Price is the cheapest money \u2014 stop anchoring it to the cheapest competitor", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Price is the cheapest money \u2014 stop anchoring it to the cheapest competitor", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Pricing is a SaaS's biggest lever, yet ~90% of products are underpriced \u2014 the founder, who knows every limitation, anchors on the cheapest competitor instead of on value delivered. The buyer only sees the problem solved."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_1__on_price_beats_1__on_acquisition___you_raise_prices_too_little_and_too_late", "label": "1% on price beats 1% on acquisition \u2014 you raise prices too little and too late", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: 1% on price beats 1% on acquisition \u2014 you raise prices too little and too late", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A 1% price improvement drives ~8\u201311% more profit (ProfitWell) on typical SaaS margins, while a 1% acquisition improvement yields ~2%. Yet 70% of executives rank acquisition as the most important growth lever \u2014 the one that pays least. Price is left untouched because of loss aversion (fear of losing what you have screams louder than the chance to gain)."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_competing_on_price_is_adverse_selection___you_filter_in_the_worst_client", "label": "Competing on price is adverse selection \u2014 you filter in the worst client", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Competing on price is adverse selection \u2014 you filter in the worst client", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Price-led copycats attract bargain hunters: they enter because you're cheapest and leave the second someone is R$1 cheaper. High churn, zero loyalty, most support tickets. Price as bait is a filter for the client you least want. Better: Kawasaki's \"Be a baker, not an eater\" \u2014 bake your own pie via niche (CRM for facial-aesthetics clinics, not another CRM) and charge 5x with no comparison. Use the giant's weight against it (jiu-jitsu): big players' blind spots are in public complaints \u2014 ReclameAqui, app stores, Google Places, communities, FB/WhatsApp groups, niche sites. Each complainer represents ~26 equally unhappy silent ones."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_make_the_middle_plan_the_one_you_actually_want_to_sell", "label": "Make the middle plan the one you actually want to sell", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Make the middle plan the one you actually want to sell", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Each plan has a behavioral job, not just a price; the plan you most want to sell should sit in the middle, flanked by a decoy below and an anchor above."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_ask_for_the_card_in_trial___but_optimize_for_the_right_icp__not_raw_conversion", "label": "Ask for the card in trial \u2014 but optimize for the right ICP, not raw conversion", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Ask for the card in trial \u2014 but optimize for the right ICP, not raw conversion", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Requiring a credit card multiplies trial-to-paid conversion but shrinks signups; the goal is the model that attracts and retains the right ICP, not the one with the highest headline conversion."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_frame_the_upgrade_as_a_loss_at_the_moment_of_value__not_a_feature_you_re_selling", "label": "Frame the upgrade as a loss at the moment of value, not a feature you're selling", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Frame the upgrade as a loss at the moment of value, not a feature you're selling", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Low upgrade rates are usually a framing-and-timing problem, not a price problem; remind users what they've already invested and what they stand to lose."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_engineer_the_comparison_frame_with_a_decoy_and_a_high_anchor___and_drop_free_from_the_top", "label": "Engineer the comparison frame with a decoy and a high anchor \u2014 and drop Free from the top", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Engineer the comparison frame with a decoy and a high anchor \u2014 and drop Free from the top", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Conversion shifts when you change the frame of comparison, not the product; equal-looking options cause delay, and showing Free first anchors everyone to zero so everything else feels expensive."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_tie_the_trial_s_end_to_value_consumed__not_the_calendar", "label": "Tie the trial's end to value consumed, not the calendar", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Tie the trial's end to value consumed, not the calendar", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Blocking access on a fixed day count (7/14/28) is a lazy rule; the billing trigger should fire on value consumption, after the user's first real win."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_order_pricing_rows_by_the_serial_position_effect__killer_feature_first__differentiator_last", "label": "Order pricing rows by the serial-position effect: killer feature first, differentiator last", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Order pricing rows by the serial-position effect: killer feature first, differentiator last", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Users don't read pricing lists linearly; attention and memory cluster on the first and last items, so feature order is itself a conversion lever."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_engineer_the_pricing_page_with_good_better_best_and_control_the_comparison", "label": "Engineer the pricing page with Good-Better-Best and control the comparison", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Engineer the pricing page with Good-Better-Best and control the comparison", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Lost LTV is rarely about price \u2014 it's analysis paralysis from a missing choice architecture. The brain is lazy and judges by relative comparison (priming + anchoring), so if you don't design the anchor, users compare you to \"nothing\" or to the cheapest competitor."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization_build_a_single_value_axis__then_tune_the_decoy_s_distance_to_your_target_plan", "label": "Build a single value axis, then tune the decoy's distance to your target plan", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "section: Build a single value axis, then tune the decoy's distance to your target plan", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A plan ladder must read as one clear progression of value; mixing quantitative and qualitative axes muddles it, and where you place the decoy's price decides which plan looks like the deal."}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization__richardrx___2026_08_26", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-26", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-26", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2092692547244007852"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization__richardrx___2026_08_03", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-03", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centric-design/references/pricing-and-monetization.md", "source_location": "link: @richardrx \u00b7 2026-08-03", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "reference_url": "https://x.com/richardrx/status/2084250609687884144"}, {"id": "_agents_skills_revenue_centric_design_references_pricing_and_monetization__richardrx___2026_07_01", "label": "@richardrx \u00b7 2026-07-01", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": ".agents/skills/revenue-centri